"H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5 (https://issforum.org/roundtables/PDF/Roundtable-XXIV-5.pdf)\nciteturn48view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://issforum.org/roundtables/PDF/Roundtable-XXIV-5.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 983\nL0@P0: H-Diplo ROUNDTABLE XXIV-5\nL1@P0: Sean McMeekin. Stalin’s War: A New History of World War II. New York: Basic Books, 2021. ISBN:\nL2@P0: 9781541672796 (hardcover, $40.00); 9781541672789 (paperback, $24.99).\nL3@P0: 26 September 2022 | https://hdiplo.org/to/RT24-5\nL4@P0: Editor: Diane Labrosse | Commissioning Editor: Thomas Maddux | Production Editor: George Fujii\nL5@P0: Contents\nL6@P0: Introduction by Warren Kimball, Rutgers University, Emeritus........................................................................................................... 2\nL7@P0: Review by Mark Edele, University of Melbourne.................................................................................................................................... 5\nL8@P0: Review by Vojin Majstorovic, University of North Texas....................................................................................................................10\nL9@P0: Review by Geoffrey Roberts, University College Cork, Emeritus....................................................................................................15\nL10@P0-1: Response by Sean McMeekin, Bard College ..........................................................................................................................................20H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL11@P1: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL12@P1: 2 | Page\nL13@P1: Introduction by Warren Kimball, Rutgers University, Emeritus\nL14@P1: Reviewers Mark Edele, Vojin Majstorovic, and Geoffrey Roberts agree that Sean McMeekin contends that Soviet leader\nL15@P1: Joseph Stalin schemed for, and purposefully tried to foment, a European war between Nazi Germany and the western\nL16@P1: (capitalist) powers, leaving the USSR (an abbreviation McMeekin seems to avoid in his book) to pick up the pieces. Hence,\nL17@P1: the Second World War is, according to McMeekin, Stalin’s War. Nor did that war end in 1945 with (in McMeekin’s words)\nL18@P1: “victory parades and flowers and kisses”; rather, “in Eastern Europe, the war lasted until 1989, in the form of Soviet military\nL19@P1: occupation” (2). The overarching theme is that the Anglo-Americans failed to seize multiple opportunities to avoid helping\nL20@P1: the Soviet Union expand its sphere of interest and/or imperial control into all of Eastern Europe, and into Greater East\nL21@P1: Asia, thus becoming a major geopolitical world power.\nL22@P1: Sometimes the devil is in the details. But the details of high policy during the Second World War were not such a devil. The\nL23@P1: high polices were the essence. Such broad themes should generate lengthy, carefully crafted historical discussions. One such\nL24@P1: does come with McMeekin’s Brobdingnagian mound of evidence about what, as early as 1994, one Russian historian labeled\nL25@P1: “an enormous flow of Lend-Lease,” especially in 1943-45.1\nL26@P1:  But explaining why that flow took place is largely bypassed by\nL27@P1: cryptic dismissals of President Franklin D. Roosevelt’s and Prime Minister Winston Churchill’s foolish naivete. McMeekin\nL28@P1: writes that “Roosevelt and Churchill missed a genuine chance in 1943 [the Katyn Forest massacre] to redefine the\nL29@P1: coalition’s still nebulous war aims in a more civilized direction” (449). But what about Roosevelt’s hardly nebulous\nL30@P1: unconditional surrender statement, made two months earlier? What about the military practicality of perhaps once again\nL31@P1: watching Stalin and German Chancellor Adolf Hitler negotiate a peace-pact? What about hopes that the wartime coalition\nL32@P1: might become a longer-term relationship?\nL33@P1: It is not the introducer’s task to settle historiographical arguments, nor my assignment (chore?) to dissect cited evidence to\nL34@P1: determine provenance, relevance, comprehensiveness, and accuracy. Yet too much, though not the essence, of these\nL35@P1: roundtable essays seems focused on just such quibbling. The reviewers point accurately to the crucial big picture, but do not\nL36@P1: effectively challenge or support McMeekin’s assumptions.\nL37@P1: There are straightforward disagreements. Geoff Roberts summarizes and quotes McMeekin arguing that the West should\nL38@P1: have confronted Stalin during the war and formed “a broad international coalition against totalitarian aggression,” an\nL39@P1: alliance that would have included “pro-Axis Hungary and Fascist Italy” (656). As for Hitler, he could have been dealt with\nL40@P1: by a peace deal that would have saved Western Europe from Soviet occupation and may even have extracted conquered\nL41@P1: Poland from his clutches (660). Apparently the assumption is that Hitler would have been overthrown. Roberts concludes\nL42@P1: that “McMeekin’s speculation that standing up to Stalin would have turned out better than standing up to Hitler is as\nL43@P1: unconvincing as his book’s distortion of the complex and contradictory history of the Second World War.” But that debate\nL44@P1: is never joined.\nL45@P1: Mark Edele concludes that\nL46@P1: “this fast-paced and well-written account argues that the historiography is too obsessed with Hitler,\nL47@P1: has underestimated the importance of Lend-Lease to the Soviet war effort, and has failed to see\nL48@P1: that the Soviet dictator was at least as bad as his German colleague. British and US support for the\nL49@P1: Soviet Union was caused by Roosevelt and Churchill’s irrational ‘Stalinophilia’ (657), not by a\nL50@P1: realistic assessment of the balance of forces. The Soviets only won because of Western military\nL51@P1: aid.”\nL52@P1: 1 David Reynolds, Warren F. Kimball, and A.O. Chubarian, Allies at War (New York: St. Martin’s, 1994): 163-64. Statistics in\nL53@P1-2: the book’s other essays bore that out.H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL54@P2: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL55@P2: 3 | Page\nL56@P2: Vojin Majstorovic’s assessment, gently but firmly, supports the other reviewers:\nL57@P2: “Stalin’s War offers stimulating insights. However, it is an ideological book, sometimes resembling\nL58@P2: a diatribe more than a scholarly study. There is nothing wrong with anti-Communism per se, but\nL59@P2: viewing the war through one ideological prism greatly simplifies the complex reality. McMeekin\nL60@P2: ignores important literature in the field, and makes numerous factual mistakes, many\nL61@P2: exaggerations, and glaring omissions, which undermine the reader’s confidence in the book’s\nL62@P2: conclusions. Any work of this length and range will contain errors, but those in this work all\nL63@P2: buttress its arguments. Thus, the book is ultimately not persuasive, even if it is interesting to read.”\nL64@P2: All the reviewers include quick summaries of those grand strategies and high politics, then engage in a war – all too often\nL65@P2: over footnotes. That could/should have served as the opening of a full debate over those grand strategies and high politics.\nL66@P2: Likewise McMeekin, who in his response joins the debate over citations. He also briefly summarizes some of those\nL67@P2: strategies, then criticizes Roosevelt and, to a slightly lesser degree, Churchill as naïve and foolish – a reputation they did not\nL68@P2: and do not have at home or elsewhere. Stalin is, it seems, brilliant and clever. Were Churchill and Roosevelt mistaken,\nL69@P2: foolish, naïve to the point of being stupid? Was their gamble that Stalin would cooperate after the war mere self-deception?\nL70@P2: Was some seventy years without a European-wide war better than what the book seems to advocate (according to the\nL71@P2: reviewers), that is, a vague Euro-American sphere of influence aimed at containing the Russians? These issues are not\nL72@P2: properly addressed in the reviews or in the author’s response.\nL73@P2: Hopefully, another roundtable will discuss those questions, perhaps in the light of how today’s Ukraine-Russia war ends up.\nL74@P2: That discussion could focus on a curious syllogism posed by McMeekin: Was the point of the war to save Western Europe\nL75@P2: from foreign subjugation, which was easy to do by striking a deal with Hitler; or to save Poland and Eastern Europe from\nL76@P2: foreign subjugation which, if so, “was an abysmal failure” (660).\nL77@P2: One could argue that this roundtable concerns Stalin’s War, not those high policy negotiations and decisions. But the\nL78@P2: validity of McMeekin’s broad thesis lies in those very high policy discussions and the decisions made by the leaders and\nL79@P2: governments of the Big Three – Churchill, Roosevelt, and Stalin. Mostly, the reviewers take a pass, while McMeekin’s\nL80@P2: responses to such matters are brief and alluded to, even mentioned, but largely in passing; never discussed in depth. To offer\nL81@P2: one example, McMeekin asserts that excessive lend-lease to Russia put the Soviet Union in a position to dominate Eastern\nL82@P2: Europe after the war. No reviewer really quarrels with that implication.\nL83@P2: The reviewers all too often passed each otherand the author like ships in the night, a bit too distracted by fascination for the\nL84@P2: details and perhaps lured by the author’s focus; bypassing each other’s arguments and much of the book’s essence.\nL85@P2: Participants:\nL86@P2: Sean McMeekin, A.B., Stanford University; M.A., Ph.D., University of California, Berkeley; also studied at University of\nL87@P2: Paris 7, Moscow State University, Humboldt University, and Mezhdunarodny Universitet, Moscow. Previously taught at\nL88@P2: Koç University, Istanbul; Bilkent University, Ankara; and Yale University and at Bard College since 2014. He is the author\nL89@P2: of Stalin’s War: A New History (Basic Books, 2017); The Russian Revolution, A New History (2017); The Ottoman\nL90@P2: Endgame. War, Revolution, and the Making of the Modern Middle East, 1908-1923 (New York: Penguin Books, 2015),\nL91@P2: winner of the Arthur Goodzeit Book Prize awarded by the New York Military Affairs Symposium (NYMAS); July 1914:\nL92@P2: Countdown to War (New York: Basic Books, 2013), which was reviewed on the cover of the New York Times Book Review;\nL93@P2: The Russian Origins of the First World War (Harvard University Press, Reprint 2013) which won the Norman B.\nL94@P2: Tomlinson Jr. Book Prize and was nominated for the Lionel Gelber Prize; The Berlin to Baghdad Express: The Ottoman\nL95@P2: Empire and Germany’s Bid for World Power, 1898–1918 (Harvard University Press/Belknap, 2010), winner of the Barbara\nL96@P2: Jelavich Book Prize; History’s Greatest Heist: The Looting of Russia by the Bolsheviks (Yale University, 2008);and The Red\nL97@P2: Millionaire: A Political Biography of Willi Münzenberg, Moscow’s Secret Propaganda Tsar in the West (Yale University Press,\nL98@P2-3: 2004) and numerous articles and book chapters. H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL99@P3: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL100@P3: 4 | Page\nL101@P3: Warren F. Kimball, author of Forged in War (William Morrow & Co., 1997), The Juggler (Princeton University Press,\nL102@P3: 1991), and books on the Morgenthau Plan and the origins of Lend-Lease, edited the three-volume collection of the\nL103@P3: Churchill-Roosevelt correspondence (with commentaries, Princeton University Press, 1984). He has published over 50\nL104@P3: essays on Churchill, Roosevelt and the era of the Second World War. Robert Treat Professor emeritus at Rutgers University,\nL105@P3: he was Pitt Professor at the University of Cambridge, 1987-1988, and Mark Clark Distinguished Professor of History at\nL106@P3: The Citadel, 2002-2004. He chaired the State Department Historical Advisory Committee for nine of his eleven years on\nL107@P3: the committee and is a former president of SHAFR. His institutional history, The US Tennis Association: Raising the Game,\nL108@P3: was published in December 2017 by the University of Nebraska Press). He was Jones Distinguished Professor at Wofford\nL109@P3: College (Spartanburg, SC) in spring 2019. He is currently active with various history organizations in promoting\nL110@P3: significantly improved access to, and much expanded declassification of, US government records held by NARA (National\nL111@P3: Archives and Records Administration).\nL112@P3: Mark Edele is Hansen Professor in History at the University of Melbourne, where he teaches Soviet history, the\nL113@P3: historiography of Stalinism, and the history of the Second World War. He is the author of Soviet Veterans of the Second\nL114@P3: World War (Oxford University Press 2008), Stalinist Society (Oxford University Press, 2011), Stalin’s Defectors (Oxford\nL115@P3: University Press, 2017), The Soviet Union. A Short History (Wiley Blackwell, 2019), Debates on Stalinism (Manchester\nL116@P3: University Press, 2020), and, with Martin Crotty and Neil Diamant, The Politics of Veteran Benefits in the Twentieth\nL117@P3: Century: A Comparative History (Cornell University Press, 2020). His latest book, Stalinism at War, will be published by\nL118@P3: Bloomsbury in September 2021.\nL119@P3: Vojin Majstorovic got his Ph.D. at the University of Toronto in 2017. He is an assistant professor of military history at the\nL120@P3: University of North Texas, where he teaches courses on World War II and the Holocaust. His manuscript, Discipline and\nL121@P3: Violence in the Red Army, 1944-1945, explores the explosion of what army officials called “military violations:” straggling,\nL122@P3: deserting, looting, and physically and sexually assaulting civilians in the last year of the war. It examines these issues from the\nL123@P3: perspectives of the political and military leaderships, the frontline troops, and European civilians. His second research\nL124@P3: project focuses on the Soviet army’s encounter with the Holocaust across East-Central Europe. His research has been\nL125@P3: published in Slavic Review, and Holocaust and Genocide Studies.\nL126@P3: Geoffrey Roberts is Emeritus Professor of History at UCC and a Member of the Royal Irish Academy. Among his books\nL127@P3: are Stalin’s Wars: From World War to Cold War, 1939-1953 (2006); Stalin’s General: The Life of Georgy Zhukov (2013\nL128@P3: winner of the Society for Military History’s Distinguished Book Award); and (with Martin Folly & Oleg Rzheshevsky),\nL129@P3: Churchill and Stalin: Comrades in Arms during the Second World War (2019). His latest book is Stalin’s Library: A Dictator\nL130@P3-4: and His Books (2022), published by Yale University Press.H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL131@P4: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL132@P4: 5 | Page\nL133@P4: Review by Mark Edele, University of Melbourne\nL134@P4: “Nothing would be worse,” said U.S. President Franklin D. Roosevelt in March 1942, “than to have the Russians collapse.”2\nL135@P4: Better to “lose New Zealand, Australia or anything else.” Why? Because “the Russians are today killing more Germans and\nL136@P4: destroying more equipment than you and I put together,” as he wrote to British Prime Minister Winston Churchill later\nL137@P4: that year. 3\nL138@P4: After some initial Cold War amnesia, historians in the English-speaking world have, by and large, accepted that the war in\nL139@P4: Europe was won by the Red Army.4 Most also agree that, on balance, this was a good outcome. While Stalin’s totalitarianism\nL140@P4: would victimize millions, it was the lesser evil.5 And until the Western allies had developed significant amphibious capability\nL141@P4: relatively late in the war, there simply was no alternative: only the Soviets could fight Germany on the continent, albeit in a\nL142@P4: global alliance with the British Empire and the United States.6\nL143@P4: Recently, however, a new generation of English-language historians have returned to the tune that it was US forces, or the\nL144@P4: valiant boys of Bomber Command, who won the war. Stalin and his soldiers, at best, were conduits for American military\nL145@P4: aid (via Roosevelt’s Lend-Lease scheme).7\nL146@P4: Stalin’s War follows both this trend and McMeekin’s track record of highly controversial histories. In short, this fast-paced\nL147@P4: and well-written account argues that the historiography is too obsessed with Hitler, has underestimated the importance of\nL148@P4: Lend-Lease to the Soviet war effort, and has failed to see that the Soviet dictator was at least as bad as his German colleague.\nL149@P4: British and U.S. support for the Soviet Union was caused by Roosevelt and Churchill’s irrational “Stalinophilia” (657), not\nL150@P4: by a realistic assessment of the balance of forces. The Soviets only won because of Western military aid.\nL151@P4: 2 A longer version of this review was first published in Inside Story (25 May 2021) https://insidestory.org.au/better-to-lose\u0002australia/. I would like to thank the editor, Peter Browne, for permission to reuse it here.\nL152@P4: 3 The Kremlin Letters: Stalin’s Wartime Correspondence with Churchill and Roosevelt, ed. David Reynolds, Vladimir Pechatnov,\nL153@P4: assist. Iskander Magadeyev, Olga Kucherenko (New Haven and London: Yale University Press, 2018), 94, 96.\nL154@P4: 4 See, for example, the classical treatment by John Erickson, Stalin’s War with Germany, 2 vols. (London: Weidenfeld &\nL155@P4: Nicolson, 1975-1983). Most recently: Wendy Z. Goldman and Donald Filtzer, Fortress Dark and Stern. The Soviet Home front during\nL156@P4: World War II (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2021).\nL157@P4: 5 Mark Edele, Sheila Fitzpatrick, and Atina Grossmann, eds., Shelter from the Holocaust: rethinking Jewish survival in the Soviet\nL158@P4: Union (Detroit: Wayne State University Press, 2017).\nL159@P4: 6 Important recent studies on the British and U.S. contributions to Allied victory include James T. Sparrow, Warfare State.\nL160@P4: World War II Americans and the Age of Big Government (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2011); David Edgerton, Britain’s War\nL161@P4: Machine. Weapons, Resources, and Experts in the Second World War (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2011); Jonathan Fennell, Fighting\nL162@P4: the People’s War. The British and Commonwealth Armies and the Second World War (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2019);\nL163@P4: and Evan Mawdsley, The War for the Seas. A Maritime History of World War II (New Haven and London: Yale University Press, 2019).\nL164@P4: 7 For a review essay arguing against this view see Mark Edele, “Who Won the Second World War and Why Should you Care?\nL165@P4: Reassessing Stalin’s War 75 Years after Victory,” Journal of Strategic Studies 43:6-7 (2019): 1039-1062, DOI:\nL166@P4-5: https://doi.org/10.1080/01402390.2019.1701443 H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL167@P5: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL168@P5: 6 | Page\nL169@P5: Such judgments are based on a caricatured picture of the literature on this war. Neither the authoritative three-volume\nL170@P5: Cambridge History of the Second World War,\nL171@P5: 8 nor Evan Mawdsley’s masterful short introduction,9 for example, apologize\nL172@P5: for Stalin or concentrate on Hitler. The role of Lend-Lease has been discussed at length, and with significant nuance,\nL173@P5: including by some of the scholars whom McMeekin quotes.10 Historians like Robert Gellately have written damning\nL174@P5: accounts of Stalin’s goals in the war and the Cold War,11 none of which are referenced in this book.\nL175@P5: If the book treats the work of other historians in a cavalier manner, it employs primary sources tendentiously. To give one\nL176@P5: example: the speech of Stalin to the graduates of the Red Army military academies on 5 May 1941 that starts the book.\nL177@P5: Uttered behind closed doors, the leader’s words were published in 1998 in a now famous collection.12 This book is not “out\nL178@P5: of print today and difficult to find,” as McMeekin claims (673), but freely available on the internet. To cite the relevant\nL179@P5: section of the source in full:\nL180@P5: Major-general of the tank forces is speaking. He proposes a toast to the peaceful Stalinist foreign\nL181@P5: policy.\nL182@P5: Comrade Stalin: Allow me a correction. The peaceful policy ensured peace for our country. A\nL183@P5: peaceful policy is a good thing. For the time being, we pursued a line of defence – until we re\u0002equipped our army, provided our army with modern means of fighting.\nL184@P5: And now, when we have reconstructed our army, saturated it with equipment for modern combat,\nL185@P5: when we have become strong — now we need to move from defence to offense.\nL186@P5: In order to defend our country, we are obliged to act in an offensive manner. From defence [we\nL187@P5: have to] transition to a military policy of offensive actions. We have to rebuild our education, our\nL188@P5: propaganda, our agitation, our press in an offensive spirit. The Red Army is a modern army, and a\nL189@P5: modern army is an offensive army. 13\nL190@P5: Historians have puzzled over these words: Did Stalin tell his soldiers that he was planning offensive war? Did Stalin have one\nL191@P5: drink too many at the reception, and make off-the cuff remarks he needed to walk back later? Or was this a pep talk to bring\nL192@P5: the troops into line with military doctrine: that any attack on the Soviet Union would be repulsed aggressively and finished\nL193@P5: quickly on the opponent’s territory? Each interpretation can be made plausible by citing other evidence, but none is\nL194@P5: provable beyond a reasonable doubt.\nL195@P5: 8 The Cambridge History of the Second World War. Volume 1: Fighting the War, ed. John Ferris, Evan Mawdsley (Cambridge:\nL196@P5: Cambridge University Press, 2015); The Cambridge History of the Second World War. Volume 2: Politics and Ideology, ed. Richard\nL197@P5: Bosworth, Joseph Maiolo (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2015); The Cambridge History of the Second World War. Volume 3:\nL198@P5: Total War: Economy, Society and Culture, ed. Michael Geyer, Adam Tooze (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2015).\nL199@P5: 9 Evan Mawdsley, World War II: A New History (Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 2020).\nL200@P5: 10 Mawdsley, Thunder in the East: the Nazi-Soviet war, 1941–1945, 2nd ed. (London: Bloomsbury, 2016).\nL201@P5: 11 Robert Gellately, Stalin’s Curse: Battling for Communism in War and Cold War (Oxford: Oxford University Press, 2016).\nL202@P5: 12 1941 god: v 2 kn. Kn. 2, ed. L. E. Reshin, V. P. Naumov et al. (Moscow: Mezhdunar. fond “Demokratiia”, 1998).\nL203@P5: 13 Document no. 437, “Vystuplenie General’nogo sekretaria TsK VKP(b) I.V. Stalina pered vypusknikami voennykh akademii\nL204@P5: RKKA v Kremle,” 5 May 1941, 1941-i god. Kniga vtoraia. Mai 1941 goda [Dok. №№ 426–511]\nL205@P5-6: https://www.alexanderyakovlev.org/fond/issues-doc/1011756H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL206@P6: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL207@P6: 7 | Page\nL208@P6: Historians are of course not obliged to tell their readers about every step they took from reading the sources to producing\nL209@P6: their interpretation. But McMeekin does something else altogether. Here is how he renders Stalin’s remarks in his book:\nL210@P6: What transpired next was so dramatic, so unexpected, that no one present ever forgot it. … Stalin\nL211@P6: leapt to his feet, cut off the poor lieutenant general, and reproached him for pushing an “out of\nL212@P6: date policy.” Stalin then moderated his tone, reassuring the officers and party bosses present that\nL213@P6: the “Soviet peace policy” … had indeed bought the Red Army time to modernize and rearm, while\nL214@P6: also allowing the USSR to “push forward in the west and north, increasing its population by\nL215@P6: thirteen million in the process.” But the days of peaceful absorption of new territory, Stalin stated\nL216@P6: forthrightly,” had come to an end. Not another foot of ground can be gained with such peaceful\nL217@P6: sentiments.”\nL218@P6: The Red Army, Stalin told its future commanders, “must get used to the idea that the era of the\nL219@P6: peace policy is at an end and that the era of widening the socialist front by force has begun.”\nL220@P6: Anyone “who failed to recognize the necessity of offensive action,” Stalin admonished, “was a\nL221@P6: bourgeois and a fool.” The defensive doctrine that had animated strategic planning and war\u0002gaming for a European conflict prior to 1941, he explained, was appropriate only for a weak,\nL222@P6: unprepared Red Army. (9)\nL223@P6: This is not history in the normal sense of the word: a disciplined, if imaginative rendering of the past constrained by what\nL224@P6: the sources say. The archival account of this speech simply does not have Stalin leap to his feet; he says nothing about an “out\nL225@P6: of date policy,” a thirteen million population increase, or a push to the west and north. No bourgeois fools and widening\nL226@P6: fronts. Where does McMeekin get this from? I checked the online version of the source, its hard copy version in the\nL227@P6: collection he cites, and the version in the Stalin archive (for which he gives a wrong file number in his footnote).14 Nowhere\nL228@P6: could I find words even close to these.\nL229@P6: A close reading of the convoluted endnote to this episode and a trip to my university’s library eventually revealed the source:\nL230@P6: an account by a German diplomat who was not present at the occasion. Published in the 1950s and cited in a notorious\nL231@P6: German revisionist history (translated into English in 1987), this version relies on interrogations of captured Soviet soldiers\nL232@P6: later in the war.15 It has been dismissed by most historians for obvious reasons, but McMeekin claims that it conforms to\nL233@P6: other eyewitness accounts (675, n. 5). I checked these, too, and found no such words. Instead, they confirm the more boring\nL234@P6: Soviet archival version.16\nL235@P6: The most telling quotations, then, the words by which McMeekin prosecutes his case against Stalin the alleged warmonger,\nL236@P6: come from an account far removed from the actual speech and published well before the Soviet archives opened. They have\nL237@P6: 14 The correct file number is RGASPI f. 558, op. 1, d. 3808, available from Stalin Digital Archive,\nL238@P6: https://www.stalindigitalarchive.com/\nL239@P6: 15 Gustav Hilger, Wir und der Kreml: deutsch-sowjetische Beziehungen 1918–1941. Erinnerungen eines deutschen Diplomaten\nL240@P6: (Frankfurt/Main: A. Metzner, 1955; 2nd ed. 1956); Ernst Topitsch, Stalin’s War: A Radical New Theory of the Origins of the Second World\nL241@P6: War (New York: St. Martin’s Press, 1987).\nL242@P6: 16 Zastol’nye rechi Stalina: dokumenty i materialy, ed. V. A. Nevezhin (Moscow, Saint-Petersburg: AIRO-XX, Dmitrii Bulanin,\nL243@P6-7: 2003), 280–281.H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL244@P7: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL245@P7: 8 | Page\nL246@P7: been called “embellishment(s)” by the most in-depth investigation, which McMeekin cites as if it supports his reading (it\nL247@P7: does not).17 This calls into question the claim that this book is based on revelations from the Soviet archives. It is not.\nL248@P7: The misleading rendering of the 1941 speech is not the only technical concern with the book. McMeekin misquotes a\nL249@P7: famous Stalin speech of 193118 as having taken place in 1928 (with a footnote leading nowhere, p. 25, fn. 2); misdates\nL250@P7: Stalin’s deportation of the Soviet Korean population (which happened in 1937 in response to the outbreak of war in Asia)\nL251@P7: to 1938, allegedly some kind of perverse victory celebration after the Battle of Lake Khasan (66); claims that Britain was\nL252@P7: “grasping for legal straws to avoid entanglement with Stalin” (111) by interpreting the phrase “European power” in the 25\nL253@P7: August 1939 Agreement of Mutual Assistance with Poland to mean Germany only (in fact this was explicitly stated in a\nL254@P7: secret protocol to the agreement)19; asserts that the April 1941 neutrality pact with Japan allowed Stalin “to concentrate\nL255@P7: everything he had on the West” (264), stripping “his Far Eastern defenses” (377) (in fact, Soviet troop strength in the east\nL256@P7: never fell below 1.1 million men, with significant military assets deployed throughout the war)20; etc. etc. His account of the\nL257@P7: role of U.S. and British aid, which is central to his argument, is a polemic that unfortunately obscures the real constellation\nL258@P7: of forces21 and is not infrequently undermined by his own evidence.\nL259@P7: Most egregiously, McMeekin cites a 1939 forgery of an alleged Stalin speech as authentic, claiming that it was recently\nL260@P7: “discovered in the Russian archives” (83). There is, indeed, a copy — in an archive holding foreign-origin documents22 —\nL261@P7: and it is a translation from a French original.23 Even the article McMeekin cites for proof of the authenticity of this\nL262@P7: document notes that “it seems to originate from an article published in the French La Revue universelle” in 1944.24 As the\nL263@P7: most accomplished political historian of Stalinism wrote in a work McMeekin cites in the book: “Most historians have never\nL264@P7: assigned much significance to this forgery. Neither the Politburo archive nor Stalin’s own files contain even circumstantial\nL265@P7: evidence of such a speech.”25\nL266@P7: 17 Jürgen Förster, Evan Mawdsley, “Hitler and Stalin in Perspective: Secret Speeches on the Eve of Barbarossa,” War in History\nL267@P7: 11:1 (January 2004): 61-103.\nL268@P7: 18 J. V. Stalin, “The Tasks of Business Executives. Speech Delivered at the First All-Union Conference of Leading Personnel of\nL269@P7: Socialist Industry,” 4 February 1931, https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/stalin/works/1931/02/04.htm\nL270@P7: 19 “Agreement of Mutual Assistance between the United Kingdom and Poland – London (1939),”\nL271@P7: https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Agreement_of_Mutual_Assistance_between_the_United_Kingdom_and_Poland-London_(1939)\nL272@P7: 20 Mark Edele, Stalinism at War. The Soviet Union in World War II (London: Bloomsbury, 2021), 106-7.\n"
